	A nationwide CenturyLink telecom outage that began on Thursday, December 27, has caused 911 service disruptions for customers across the United States. It s unclear how many individuals have been affected by the outage. Service was restored to a large number of previously impacted areas in the central and eastern United States. Outages have persisted in parts of Denver, Portland, Seattle, Los Angeles, Salt Lake City, Albuquerque, Boise, Chicago, Phoenix, and Denver, as well as a large portion of the state of Washington. Additionally, some Verizon Wireless customers reported similar disruptions in New Mexico and Montana as CenturyLink helps to handle wireless network traffic for Verizon and other carriers. The outage is affecting CenturyLink internet, phone, and television services.     

	Assessment: The Federal Communications Commission (FCC) has launched an investigation into the outage. As of this writing there have been no reports on the cause of the outage; although an intentional attack has not yet been ruled out. The FCC has previously fined CenturyLink $16 million following a similar outage that was deemed preventable in April of 2014. Though internet and phone outages happen regularly across the country, the majority of those outages are not prolonged and do not affect 911 service. Continued 911 service disruptions can be especially debilitating for local communities as it makes it difficult for emergency services to respond in a timely manner to ongoing emergencies. Local officials are advising those CenturyLink customers affected by the outage to look up the local ten-digit non-emergency phone numbers in case emergency services are needed.

Headline_Details:
CenturyLink Outage Enters its Second Day as 911 Service Remains Disrupted Across the U.S. Normal 
